{100}{}{You see Jenny Stahl, who serves at the Brass Lantern.}
{101}{}{A woman in her twenties with her sleeves rolled and a ladle within reach. She has the steady look of the one sober person in a large family.}

# Greetings: 200 first, 201 later, 202 bomb disarmed, 203 Simms dead.
{200}{}{Brass Lantern. Food's hot, water's clean, and nobody here sells your secrets back to you. Sit on anything that holds.}
{201}{}{Back again. The iguana's fresh. Well. Fresh as iguana gets.}
{202}{}{You're the one who pulled that bomb's teeth. Lunch is the usual price, but I'll mean the smile.}
{203}{}{We buried the sheriff, and people still have to eat. Seems wrong, but there it is. What'll you have?}

{210}{}{What's on the counter today?}
{211}{}{Who runs this place?}
{212}{}{How do you stack up against Moriarty's?}
{213}{}{Anyone in town I should steer clear of?}
{214}{}{That's all. Thanks.}
{215}{}{Food!}
{216}{}{Me go.}
{217}{}{What's in the stew?}

{220}{}{My brother Andy keeps the books, my brother Leo keeps the customers talking, and I keep the two of them off each other's throats. It's a family business.}
{221}{}{He sells drink, and then he sells what people said while they were drunk. We sell lunch. Guess which one the town could live without.}
{222}{}{Jericho. He was a raider, and he says he retired. A man doesn't retire from that. He only gets slower at it. If he leans on my counter again, he gets the ladle.}
{223}{}{What did he do?}
{224}{}{Ask him when he's drunk. He'll tell you, and he'll smile while he does it, and then you'll know what I know. I don't serve him. That's all the law I've got.}
{225}{}{Mostly what it says on the board. The rest is a family secret, and the family isn't sure either.}

{230}{}{Anything else? The pot doesn't stir itself.}
{231}{}{One more question.}
{232}{}{Point at it. That one? Good choice. It's the only choice, but it's a good one.}
{233}{}{More food!}
